<section image-layout="blank" id="57sLOYhS9H"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="5nLmNAYbYI"><p role="eyebrow">Three-era comparison · Dominant tendencies</p><h2>What changes across the three eras?</h2><table col-widths="[16,28,28,28]" borders="all" striped="false"><tr><th>Dimension</th><th background-color="palette-2">Print · Fixed instance</th><th background-color="palette-3"><span text-color="#171A1C">Digital · Coded state</span></th><th background-color="accent">Computational · Generated variation</th></tr><tr><th>Production</th><td>Composed instance</td><td>Coded state</td><td>Generated variation</td></tr><tr><th>Distribution</th><td>Editions and physical circulation</td><td>Network access and maintained versions</td><td>On-demand synthesis</td></tr><tr><th>Audience</th><td>Reader and interpreter</td><td>User who changes system state</td><td>Participant and evaluator</td></tr><tr><th>Authorship</th><td>Makers and production trades</td><td>Collaborative technical stacks</td><td>People, data, models, and interfaces</td></tr></table><aside icon="false"><p font-size="18pt">These are overlapping analytical lenses, not sealed eras. Each newer system carries older forms—and their assumptions—inside it.</p></aside></grid-item></grid></section>